00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.480
Now back to the video. Today we are&nbsp;
talking about machine translation models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.800
like the ones behind Google Translate.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.040
As you’ve maybe already&nbsp;
noticed with Google Translate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:17.280
while for some language pairs like English and&nbsp;
German, the translation quality is impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.120
for rarer language pairs, the&nbsp;
translation quality can be lower.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.800
Although, we cannot really demonstrate this for&nbsp;
German-Romanian or other language pairs I speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.480
since the algorithms are probably&nbsp;
based on transformer models and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.440
improved their performance quite a lot lately.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.880
But the bad news is, that some languages&nbsp;
are not even covered by Google Translate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.240
One reason for this is that it is hard&nbsp;
to find enough training data for them.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.640
And Google Translate is just an example, the&nbsp;
problem that some languages work better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.480
others, is well known for machine translation&nbsp;
systems, not just for Google Translate:

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.800
Languages for which you can find a lot&nbsp;
of text on the internet (so high-resource&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.040
languages) like English, German, French,&nbsp;
Spanish, have high machine translation quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.680
because you know the motto of neural&nbsp;
networks: the more data, the better.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.520
But there are lots and lots&nbsp;
of low-resource languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.600
for which you can find very little&nbsp;
or no data on the world wide web.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.800
Just so you get an idea, there are more than&nbsp;
7000 languages spoken in the world today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.000
but machine translation systems have been built&nbsp;
for around 100 languages so far, for which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.760
data collection was easy and where there is&nbsp;
more economical incentive to cover a language.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.920
As the paper points out, we cite&nbsp;
“Despite high speaker populations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:46.560
languages spoken in Africa, South and South-East&nbsp;
Asia and indigenous languages of the Americas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.760
are relatively under-served. For example,&nbsp;
Google Translate supports Frisian, Maltese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:59.120
Icelandic, and Corsican, each with fewer than&nbsp;
1M L1 speakers, but not (up until this work)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.520
Bhojpuri (~51M speakers), Oromo&nbsp;
(~24M speakers) [and so on]”.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.160
So the authors refer to these languages&nbsp;
as long-tail languages, “since the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.920
scarcity requires the application&nbsp;
of machine learning techniques that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.880
can generalize beyond the languages for&nbsp;
which ample training data is available.”

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.760
So, the idea of this paper is: if&nbsp;
we want to go far beyond hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.600
of languages to reach the order&nbsp;
of magnitude of the thousands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.720
the authors need to crawl a dataset that&nbsp;
captures the long tail too. Easy, right?

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.360
No, it turned out to be&nbsp;
much harder than I thought.&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.200
No wonder this paper is such a long one,&nbsp;
describing a lot of the problems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.000
can arise when trying to collect&nbsp;
data for low-resource languages.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.720
But first, what kind of data are we&nbsp;
talking about? The training data of modern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.760
neural machine translation systems is a mix of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:56.320
parallel and monolingual data. Parallel data&nbsp;
is key for machine translation because it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.280
that for each sentence in a language, we have&nbsp;
the same sentence in a different language.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.360
Clearly, this data should be the most&nbsp;
valuable for learning to translate.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.920
But imagine, that pairs of sentences in&nbsp;
different languages are not as cheap and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.720
easy to get as monolingual text where&nbsp;
we have sentences just in one language.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.600
Researchers (at Google, not me)&nbsp;
have parallel data for 112 languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.280
consisting of 25 billion sentence pairs.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.680
To get to a thousand languages, the&nbsp;
parallel data strategy cannot scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.440
(at least not for now) and machine&nbsp;
translation systems that train on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.040
any available parallel data, can also&nbsp;
train on additional monolingual data.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.120
The crazy part is that through&nbsp;
back translation and self-training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.520
they learn to translate between languages&nbsp;
for which there is no parallel data!

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.960
But let’s not get ahead of us&nbsp;
now, our point here is, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.840
to extend machine translation&nbsp;
to a thousand languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.960
the best bet of the authors is to collect&nbsp;
monolingual data of a thousand languages.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.400
Then to train a model on the parallel&nbsp;
data they already have and also on all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.640
monolingual sentences and bam: they can enable&nbsp;
machine translation for a thousand languages!

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.280
Then, great! Let’s gather some monolingual&nbsp;
data for more than a thousand languages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.520
It should be easy, right? Right?

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.280
No, it is not. Kreutzer and collaborators&nbsp;
(2022) show that data gathering strategies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.520
work for high-resource languages,&nbsp;
are gathering way too much noise for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.040
low-resource languages and therefore&nbsp;
cannot be applied in the same way.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.840
The problem here is, that to&nbsp;
gather data for a language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.880
one first needs to identify whether text from&nbsp;
the internet is written in that language.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.960
To do this at internet-level, this&nbsp;
language identification should be fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.720
so using a transformer model&nbsp;
is maybe not the best idea.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.720
Even worse, after gathering the data, storing&nbsp;
it before filtering can be challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.040
(and this is Google speaking with a&nbsp;
lot of servers and data storage!).

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.840
Ok, so how did the authors proceed?&nbsp;
By starting with the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:08.960
which is determining the 1,745 languages&nbsp;
the authors would be interested in scraping.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.920
This number is of course an upper bound of what&nbsp;
will come after data gathering and filtering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.480
since it is too optimistic to&nbsp;
think that for all these languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.040
the crawled text would have high enough quality.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.520
The list of 1,745 languages is not&nbsp;
containing 83 languages for which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.240
the authors already have a lot of data.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.440
So, they move on by training a simple and&nbsp;
lightweight language identification model.&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.440
It is important for this one to be fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.240
because it will look at enormous amounts&nbsp;
of text, at virtually the whole internet.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:49.280
This model is a CLD3 model which is a feed&nbsp;
forward neural network working on text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.760
represented as a bag of characters,&nbsp;
basically counting how many times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.240
certain character ngrams, so groups&nbsp;
of characters occur in the text.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.160
Maybe you wonder why not bag of words, but&nbsp;
bag of characters? Well, the authors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.040
working with thousands of languages, where for&nbsp;
a lot of them, it is not straightforward how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.320
to split text into words, since not all&nbsp;
languages are as benevolent as English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:20.480
where words are separated by spaces (and sentences&nbsp;
are separated by clear punctuation signs).

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.080
With character ngrams, the model can be applied&nbsp;
on languages which are harder to tokenize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.800
They train this model on, we cite&nbsp;
“on an aggregation of proprietary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.400
and publicly available text corpora, with&nbsp;
an average of 800K tokens per language”.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.240
But surprise! The authors did not use this&nbsp;
model to start crawling the dataset just yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:49.120
but just to test on which of the 1,745&nbsp;
languages, the model performs well enough.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.880
Because on some languages, the language&nbsp;
classification error of the language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:59.840
identifier is just too high, so they drop&nbsp;
these languages, keeping 1,629 of them.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.520
On these 1,629 remaining languages,&nbsp;
they train another CLD3 model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:12.720
Why didn’t they use the other CLD3 model&nbsp;
they just trained on 1,745 languages?

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.240
We do not know for sure, as the paper is&nbsp;
visibly written by many authors. It looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.840
like each single one of them did a part in this&nbsp;
pipeline and when writing it up, they sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.360
failed to connect the dots between the different&nbsp;
paper sections to motivate some steps properly.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.120
We can only think of the obvious&nbsp;
reason of retraining another CLD3 model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.680
on just 1,629 languages because&nbsp;
a classification model with less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.600
output possibilities, has a better&nbsp;
chance to not confuse languages.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:46.240
Ok, great! With this lightweight&nbsp;
language identification model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:52.720
the authors finally crawl the internet to&nbsp;
find text in one of these 1,629 languages.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.040
Do you think they are done?

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:00.080
Not so fast! The problems are just starting&nbsp;
because there is a lot of garbage in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:05.600
scraped text, like for example random sentences&nbsp;
of non-linguistic content that is fooling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.480
language identification model into thinking&nbsp;
it’s one of the languages of interest.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.240
So the authors discard sentences&nbsp;
whose language does not correspond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.480
to the language of the whole document.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.120
And they remove the sentences&nbsp;
which do not contain enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.160
words that are most frequent&nbsp;
for the predicted language.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.920
There is even more filtering going on&nbsp;
here with clustering of languages which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.560
are varieties of the same language and&nbsp;
more filtering based on token frequency.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.920
But there is still some filtering to do. Now&nbsp;
that a raw corpus is already gathered and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:42.080
data amounts are bounded, the authors use a more&nbsp;
sophisticated and slower language identification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.720
model to make even better predictions on&nbsp;
what the language of each sentence is.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:52.400
Yes, you guessed correctly: this more complex&nbsp;
language identifier is transformer-based!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.760
After even more filtering with this&nbsp;
identifier and sentence deduplication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.640
some languages still needed more&nbsp;
filtering with language-specific fixes.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:09.200
For example, there were funny occurrences where in&nbsp;
Scottish Gaelic, 570M sentences contained a token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.400
which means download. Or Cree that&nbsp;
had almost only Lorem Ipsum sentences.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:23.040
And this was it! It was simple enough, right?&nbsp;
No, it wasn't easy at all. The authors let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.200
volunteers (which are native speakers,&nbsp;
but also non-speakers doing detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:33.440
work) audit 72 languages to estimate how&nbsp;
much usable data the authors end up with.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:39.280
The median score is 80%, which speaks a bit for&nbsp;
the difficulty of the problem of scraping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:45.200
right data and applying all the right filters&nbsp;
for such a large and diverse set of languages.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.640
Ok, so now that the dataset more or less stands,&nbsp;
the authors are ready to train machine translation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:56.320
systems on it. But not on all of it, but on that&nbsp;
part of the languages that have enough data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:01.440
which is 25,000 sentences before&nbsp;
the deduplication step in filtering.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:06.400
Yes, you heard right: before the deduplication,&nbsp;
not after. We do not know why, this is again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.320
an unmotivated design choice that&nbsp;
the paper does not care to explain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:16.880
So the authors choose 1057 languages which&nbsp;
have 25K sentences before deduplication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.560
add the 83 high-resource languages they&nbsp;
already had data for to set up an impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:28.080
set of monolingual data. But wait, there&nbsp;
is more, namely the 25 billion sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:34.240
pairs of parallel data of translation&nbsp;
to and from English for 112 languages.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:38.160
So it’s just now that the machine&nbsp;
translation systems are ready to train.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:44.240
For the modelling part, there is not really&nbsp;
novelty from this paper (which is nothing bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:49.520
since the main contribution is the dataset and the&nbsp;
engineering work to get models to train on it).

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.160
So their modelling choices are pretty&nbsp;
standard: the model learns to translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:58.720
the 112 languages with the help of&nbsp;
the parallel data that is available.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.440
But as a second objective, it is&nbsp;
simultaneously trained on the monolingual data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.000
so on the 1057 + 83 languages with the MASS&nbsp;
training objective, which basically means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:16.160
that random spans of text are masked out and&nbsp;
the model needs to predict the MASKed tokens.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:22.320
It is like BERT, but instead of masking individual&nbsp;
tokens, here we mask entire groups of words.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.240
The idea of this simultaneous training&nbsp;
on parallel and monolingual data is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.120
the parallel data teaches the model to&nbsp;
translate between around a 100 languages,&nbsp;

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.920
the masked language modelling&nbsp;
on monolingual data teaches the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.960
model something useful about language in general.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.120
Such that at the end of training, the model is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.920
able to do not only translate between&nbsp;
the 100 languages with parallel data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.160
but also do rough translations between&nbsp;
English and the rest of the languages.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:53.040
Which comes in handy, because now the authors&nbsp;
use another established technique in machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:56.640
translation, called self training via&nbsp;
back translation. Meaning that the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:00.800
can continue training in a second stage&nbsp;
by using the two objectives we know so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:06.640
far – translation and masked language modelling&nbsp;
at span level – but also train to translate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:11.920
sentence translation pairs that previous&nbsp;
checkpoints of the model have generated.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:17.280
So here we are, with trained models&nbsp;
on more than a thousand languages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:22.400
There are quite a number of ablations the authors&nbsp;
do, but we will focus on two kinds of experiments:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.360
the first experiment is comparing a&nbsp;
1.5 Billion and a 6 Billion parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:32.560
model trained on a combination of 112&nbsp;
languages with parallel data and 206&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:37.200
languages with monolingual data. So we&nbsp;
are not yet at a 1000 languages here.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.320
Unsurprisingly, the more parameters models&nbsp;
have, the better they memorize / ahm, we mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:47.920
capture the diversity of all these languages, at&nbsp;
least when it comes to translating into English.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.720
But the most interesting part was when&nbsp;
the authors compared a model trained on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:58.560
200 languages with one trained on 1000&nbsp;
languages: What would we expect here?

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:03.920
Well, of course the model trained with 1000&nbsp;
languages can translate between more languages!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:08.880
Duh! But the surprising part is that&nbsp;
adding so many as a thousand languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:13.200
from which most are low-resource&nbsp;
and noisier in quality, does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.240
interfere with the translation performance&nbsp;
of most high resource languages. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:25.280
bit unexpected because previous work by Siddhant&nbsp;
and collaborators made us think that adding many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:31.760
low resource languages in the dataset of&nbsp;
monolingual sentences, can hurt model performance.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:37.600
The explanation of the authors is not necessarily&nbsp;
proven or exact, but they hypothesized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.640
that after a certain number of languages,&nbsp;
the probability for each added language to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:49.760
be similar to an already existing one, increases.&nbsp;
In other words, it might seem complex to translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:54.640
between a thousand languages, if they do&nbsp;
not have any similarities. But they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:00.320
which explains the observed effect. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
this paper does not have a full suite of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:05.280
experiments that gradually increases the number&nbsp;
of languages from the hundreds to the thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:09.760
to see at what language number this effect&nbsp;
starts to appear and whether the improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.520
is continuous or makes a discontinuous jump.
 
Now thinking a bit further about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:19.280
what would be interesting to know&nbsp;
in this highly multilingual regime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:24.800
is whether languages with a lot of resources&nbsp;
that were seen more often by the model, somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:30.640
affect or culturally imprint the low resource&nbsp;
languages. More concretely, we are wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:36.080
whether a multilingual model that has seen a lot&nbsp;
of English but not so much Romanian would “think”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:42.640
more in English than in “Romanian”, introducing&nbsp;
English specificities in Romanian translations.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:47.520
But now it’s time to conclude this video by&nbsp;
noting that the contributions of this paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.800
are great! The thing to be unhappy about&nbsp;
is that the release of this paper does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.840
not come with a release of the data or&nbsp;
of the models. We guess that Google has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:02.640
little incentive to open source the research&nbsp;
they base their Google Translate product on.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:08.560
Speaking of open source, look at Meta’s&nbsp;
response to this paper: They train a 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:14.160
language machine translation system and open&nbsp;
source it! I guess Google wins at the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:20.160
of languages, but Meta AI wins at making its&nbsp;
research more accessible to a wider public.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:24.000
So what are your thoughts on&nbsp;
this? Let us know in the comments.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:27.760
We hope you learned something today&nbsp;
and hope to see you in a next video!

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.840
Okay, bye!

